      "final_text": "Done; `check_candidate` returns `VERIFIED` and `verify_passport` reproduces it.\n\n**Fix.** The bug was in the given-out path: the requested amount was converted to 18-decimal units with `mulDown` (round down), so 17 wei of osETH priced as 17 units instead of the correct rounded-up 18, undercharging the trader. The new `BalancerSwap.onSwap` in [candidate/Solution.lean](<workspace>/candidate/Solution.lean):\n- given-out: upscales with `scaleUp` (round up), reverts unless the rounded-up amount is strictly below the pool's scaled balance, then charges `ceil(ceil(P\u00b71e18/rate_in)\u00b71e18/(1e18\u2212fee))`;\n- given-in: prices `netInDown` exactly and returns the payout rounded down.\n\nEvery rounding now favors the pool.\n\n**Proofs.** All 8 required theorems proved with the exact locked statements, no `sorry`, only allowed imports (`BalancerSwap.Spec`, `ProofForgeFin.Rounding`) and permitted axioms. Key lemmas: `raw_le_of_scale_lt` (rounded-up comparison implies raw-amount comparison), `ceilDiv2_bound` (double-ceiling charge is within one unit per rounding), and `add_floor_le` / `sub_floor_ge` / `out_new_in_ge` (scaled balances after settlement dominate the math's ideal balances), from which `SwapKeepsValue` follows via `WellBehaved` soundness + monotonicity, and `BatchKeepsValue` by induction.\n\n**Verification.** `check_candidate`: all 8 requirements `VERIFIED`, accepted by all six kernels, 2001/2001 property cases pass. Passport `.proofforge/passport-002.json` (`cd1edc4e\u2026`), reproduced by `verify_passport`.",
